tent": "engenn7le10ob05re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "selected": 0}]}], "stimuli_ids": [], "template_override": 1, "type": "DWS", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " Write an advice column on how to deal with Internet problems."}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Take Away"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 11}], "type": "scholar", "id": 0, "stimuli": [{"contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "_data": [], "template_override": 1}, {"contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"images": [{"text": "", "time": "0", "id": 0, "image": "engenn7le10ob05re2img05.jpg"}, {"text": "Do you remember how life was before the Internet? <br/><br/>If you are a digital native like me, it seems almost impossible to imagine a time when the answer to any question was more than a few clicks away. However, does that mean you always find what you're looking for online? How do you process all the information that lies at your fingertips?", "time": "100", "id": 1, "image": "engenn7le10ob05re2img05.jpg"}, {"text": "No matter what type of information you're looking for, the first thing you should check is the reliability and accuracy of the source you're using. How correct is the information on the website? Internet sites are used for a variety of purposes, including advertising, informing, and reporting. The second thing you should check is the purpose of the information presented and the author's credentials.", "time": "21000", "id": 2, "image": "engenn7le10ob05re2img02.jpg"}, {"text": "If you are doing research in order to write a scientific article, look for articles with a similar purpose and make sure the author is a legitimate source in the scientific community. For example, you could look at the author's background, education, professional qualifications, job title, and the legitimacy of the organization the author is writing for. Reliable scientific journals, for example, are always peer-reviewed. I wouldn't trust an online source if recognized experts in the field have not reviewed the articles for accuracy. You wouldn't want to rely on inaccurate information by accident!", "time": "49500", "id": 3, "image": "engenn7le10ob05re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn7le10ob05re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn7le10ob05re2img05.jpg"}}], "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"contents": [], "template_override": 1, "type": "static", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}, {"contents": [{"type": "p", "value": "Do you remember how life was before the Internet? <br/><br/>If you are a digital native like me, it seems almost impossible to imagine a time when the answer to any question was more than a few clicks away. However, does that mean you always find what you're looking for online? How do you process all the information that lies at your fingertips?<br><br>\n\nNo matter what type of information you're looking for, the first thing you should check is the reliability and accuracy of the source you're using. How correct is the information on the website? Internet sites are used for a variety of purposes, including advertising, informing, and reporting. The second thing you should check is the purpose of the information presented and the author's credentials.<br><br>\n\nIf you are doing research in order to write a scientific article, look for articles with a similar purpose and make sure the author is a legitimate source in the scientific community. For example, you could look at the author's background, education, professional qualifications, job title, and the legitimacy of the organization the author is writing for. Reliable scientific journals, for example, are always peer-reviewed. I wouldn't trust an online source if recognized experts in the field have not reviewed the articles for accuracy. You wouldn't want to rely on inaccurate information by accident!"}], "template_override": 1, "type": "text", "_data": [], "id": 3, "title": ""}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}]}]}